WEBVTT
Language: en

1
00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:15.667
The GTFS Schedule reference publishes a sample feed for an invented Demo Transit Authority: 11 CSV files, each one table with a header row. This explainer reads the seven listed here. Each count is the file’s rows below its header.

2
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:39.533
Each box is one file, with the columns this explainer uses. Each line joins two files by the column named on it. The marks at a line’s ends say how many rows of the table at that end can match one row at the other end; the list on the right reads them.

3
00:00:42.500 --> 00:01:02.767
routes.txt gives a route its names, vehicle type, colours and web page, and nothing about when it runs. Each row of trips.txt names one route in route_id, so a route has zero or more trips. The reference defines a route as “a group of trips that are displayed to riders as a single service”.

4
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:22.333
stop_times.txt joins trips to stops: one row for each stop a trip makes, in stop_sequence order, with its times. AB1 leaves Nye County Airport at 8:00 and reaches Bullfrog at 8:10. A trip listed in frequencies.txt repeats through the day on a headway.

5
00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:43.033
A trip’s service_id must match a row of calendar.txt, of calendar_dates.txt, or both. calendar.txt sets weekdays between a start and an end date; calendar_dates.txt adds or removes single dates. Here FULLW (7 trips) is in calendar.txt and calendar_dates.txt, and WE (4 trips) is in calendar.txt alone.

6
00:01:44.900 --> 00:02:01.033
Monday 4 June 2007 lies between start_date and end_date for both services. FULLW has monday = 1, but calendar_dates.txt removes it on 20070604 with exception_type 2, which overrides the weekly pattern; WE has monday = 0. So none of the 11 trips runs.

7
00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:25.167
One bar per trip, grouped by service_id and labelled with its route. A trip that runs once spans its first departure to its last arrival; a trip in frequencies.txt spans the hours its vehicles leave the first stop. Grey bars, marked “no service”, do not run that day. On 3 June FULLW and WE run.

8
00:02:26.567 --> 00:02:43.067
The same chart, edited for 4 June: each trip that starts or stops running gets a new label and colour, 22 edits in all. Eleven trips with service_id FULLW and WE stop. All 5 routes are still in routes.txt; none has a trip running.

9
00:02:44.133 --> 00:03:01.400
A departure is a stop_times row at the stop that is not its trip’s last. On 4 June no trip runs, so no row counts. The card lists what calendar.txt alone would send from Nye County Airport on a Monday; calendar_dates.txt removes FULLW for the day.
